  <section class="section wrap service-page__body">
    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">Start in the room</h2>
    <p>
      Drapery either works with a room's architecture or argues with it. Where the rod goes. How
      far the panels stack back when they are open, and how much wall they take up doing it. Those
      calls are what make a room read taller and finished instead of merely covered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Amy works it out standing in the room, at the hour the light matters most. A north-facing
      sitting room and a west-facing dining room want different weights and different linings even
      when the windows are identical.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">What the fabric will actually do</h2>
    <p>
      Fabric comes from Schumacher and the other lines I represent, chosen for behavior rather
      than for how it photographs on a memo sample. How it hangs. What it looks like after a
      season of being drawn every evening. A linen that is gorgeous flat can fall like a wet towel
      at length, and a pattern that reads beautifully in the hand can vanish the moment it is
      gathered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lining is settled in the same conversation. Interlining adds weight and body and makes a
      lighter fabric hang like a heavier one. Blackout belongs in bedrooms and media rooms.
      Sun-facing rooms need protection from fade. These are the choices that decide whether a
      treatment still looks right in five years.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">Pleats and hardware</h2>
    <p>
      Pleat style sets the character. Euro pleats read cleaner and more current. French pleats hold
      a deeper fold and lean traditional. Ripplefold moves as one continuous wave, which suits a
      wide contemporary span. Goblet pleats belong in the rooms that can carry them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hardware gets chosen alongside the fabric, never after. Bracket projection and return decide
      how the finished treatment sits against the wall, and a rod picked for looks alone will bow
      under interlined panels. Trim is where a treatment stops looking bought and starts looking
      made. A contrast band down a leading edge. A return that closes tight to the frame so no
      light sneaks around the side.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display service-page__subhead">A decorator does not make anything</h2>
    <p>
      Amy sews the drapery herself and installs it herself. Nothing goes out to a workroom that
      never saw the room, and nobody hangs it who was not part of the decisions. Panels are dressed
      and trained on installation day so the folds fall correctly from the start, and the treatment
      is left looking the way it should look every day after.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hard to photograph, obvious the second you walk in: the hem hangs level, the pattern matches
      across panels, the stack clears the glass. Precise work, done by someone who knew what the
      room needed before anything was cut.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap contact-band bg-ground">
    <p class="type-display contact-band__line">Ready to start your project?</p>
    <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-solid">Schedule a Consultation</a>
  </section>
